{"subjectId":"sql","id":"recursive-ctes","title":"Recursive CTEs","group":"Subqueries, CTEs & Windows","order":35,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>WITH RECURSIVE</code> lets a CTE refer to itself — the tool for hierarchies (org charts, categories, bills of materials), graphs and generated series.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-repeat\"></i>How it works</h3>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Anchor member</div><div class=\"step-desc\">A normal <code>SELECT</code> that produces the starting rows.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\"><code>UNION ALL</code></div><div class=\"step-desc\">Separates the anchor from the recursive part.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Recursive member</div><div class=\"step-desc\">A <code>SELECT</code> that joins the CTE's <strong>previous</strong> rows to produce the next rows.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">4</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Stop</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Recursion ends when the recursive member returns no new rows.</div></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-numbers\"></i>Generating a series</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>WITH RECURSIVE n(x) AS (\n    SELECT 1                    -- anchor\n    UNION ALL\n    SELECT x + 1 FROM n         -- recursive step\n    WHERE x &lt; 5                 -- termination\n)\nSELECT x, x * x AS square FROM n;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>x | square\n--+-------\n1 | 1\n2 | 4\n3 | 9\n4 | 16\n5 | 25</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- a month calendar Jan–Apr 2024, then orders per month including empty months\nWITH RECURSIVE months(m) AS (\n    SELECT '2024-01-01'\n    UNION ALL\n    SELECT date(m, '+1 month') FROM months WHERE m &lt; '2024-04-01'\n)\nSELECT strftime('%Y-%m', m) AS month, COUNT(o.order_id) AS orders\nFROM months\nLEFT JOIN orders o ON strftime('%Y-%m', o.order_date) = strftime('%Y-%m', m)\nGROUP BY m\nORDER BY m;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>month   | orders\n--------+-------\n2024-01 | 2\n2024-02 | 3\n2024-03 | 3\n2024-04 | 0</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hierarchy\"></i>Walking a hierarchy: the org chart</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>WITH RECURSIVE org(emp_id, name, manager_id, level, path) AS (\n    SELECT emp_id, name, manager_id, 0, name\n    FROM employees\n    WHERE manager_id IS NULL                            -- start at the top (Asha)\n    UNION ALL\n    SELECT e.emp_id, e.name, e.manager_id, org.level + 1, org.path || ' &gt; ' || e.name\n    FROM employees e\n    JOIN org ON e.manager_id = org.emp_id               -- children of the previous level\n)\nSELECT substr('            ', 1, level * 2) || name AS org_chart, level, path\nFROM org\nORDER BY path;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>org_chart | level | path\n----------+-------+---------------------\nAsha      | 0     | Asha\n  Dev     | 1     | Asha &gt; Dev\n  Priya   | 1     | Asha &gt; Priya\n    Arjun | 2     | Asha &gt; Priya &gt; Arjun\n    Neha  | 2     | Asha &gt; Priya &gt; Neha\n  Ravi    | 1     | Asha &gt; Ravi\n    Karan | 2     | Asha &gt; Ravi &gt; Karan\n    Meera | 2     | Asha &gt; Ravi &gt; Meera\n  Vikram  | 1     | Asha &gt; Vikram\n    Sara  | 2     | Asha &gt; Vikram &gt; Sara</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-up\"></i>Walking up: all managers of one employee</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>WITH RECURSIVE chain(emp_id, name, manager_id, hops) AS (\n    SELECT emp_id, name, manager_id, 0 FROM employees WHERE name = 'Meera'\n    UNION ALL\n    SELECT m.emp_id, m.name, m.manager_id, c.hops + 1\n    FROM employees m\n    JOIN chain c ON m.emp_id = c.manager_id\n)\nSELECT hops, name FROM chain ORDER BY hops;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>hops | name\n-----+------\n0    | Meera\n1    | Ravi\n2    | Asha</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- total salary cost under each manager (all levels), a \"subtree sum\"\nWITH RECURSIVE reports(boss_id, emp_id) AS (\n    SELECT manager_id, emp_id FROM employees WHERE manager_id IS NOT NULL\n    UNION ALL\n    SELECT r.boss_id, e.emp_id\n    FROM reports r JOIN employees e ON e.manager_id = r.emp_id\n)\nSELECT b.name AS manager, COUNT(*) AS total_reports, SUM(e.salary) AS team_cost\nFROM reports r\nJOIN employees b ON b.emp_id = r.boss_id\nJOIN employees e ON e.emp_id = r.emp_id\nGROUP BY b.emp_id, b.name\nORDER BY total_reports DESC, b.emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>manager | total_reports | team_cost\n--------+---------------+----------\nAsha    | 9             | 787000\nRavi    | 2             | 190000\nPriya   | 2             | 142000\nVikram  | 1             | 85000</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>A cycle in the data (A manages B, B manages A) makes the recursion infinite. Guard with a depth limit (<code>WHERE level &lt; 20</code>), track visited ids in a path string, or use <code>UNION</code> instead of <code>UNION ALL</code> (stops when no <em>new</em> rows appear). PostgreSQL 14+ has a <code>CYCLE</code> clause; SQL Server stops at <code>MAXRECURSION</code> (default 100).</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Keyword differences: PostgreSQL, MySQL 8 and SQLite require <code>WITH RECURSIVE</code>; SQL Server and Oracle use plain <code>WITH</code> (Oracle also has the older <code>CONNECT BY PRIOR</code>).</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you query hierarchical data in SQL?</summary><div><p>Store a parent pointer (<code>manager_id</code>) and use a recursive CTE: the anchor selects the root(s), the recursive member joins the table to the CTE on <code>child.parent_id = cte.id</code>, carrying a <code>level</code> and <code>path</code>. Alternatives for read-heavy trees: materialised path, nested sets, closure tables, or PostgreSQL's <code>ltree</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}